<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
            http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.29.xsd">

    <!--
        branches.address becomes TEXT (S4).

        WHY. The column was jsonb and BranchService wrote the request body's `address` string into
        it verbatim, so PostgreSQL rejected every value that was not valid JSON and the driver's
        DataIntegrityViolationException surfaced to the owner as 409 CONFLICT — "This conflicts with
        existing data". Measured live through the gateway, 2026-08-12:

            {"address":"12 Khayaban-e-Iqbal, F-7 Markaz, Islamabad"} -> 409
            {"address":"Islamabad"}                                  -> 409
            {"address":"\"12 Khayaban-e-Iqbal\""}                    -> 200, persisted

        The only accepted input was one that happened to be valid JSON — a bare quoted string. No
        human types quote marks into an address box, and there was no other correct input to guess.

        WHY TEXT AND NOT "SERIALISE THE STRING". Nothing in this product writes or reads a
        structured address. The DTOs declare a bare String in both directions, /app/settings renders
        one plain text input labelled "Address", and pos-service's receipt header renders whatever
        it is given as lines. The jsonb type asserted a structure that no writer produced. Wrapping
        the string in JSON on write would have preserved that false claim and left the next writer
        of this column one plain sentence away from the same 409.

        THE DATA. Three shapes exist in user_db today and all three are converted here rather than
        left for a reader to cope with:
          · JSON string  "12 Khayaban-e-Iqbal"                        -> 12 Khayaban-e-Iqbal
          · JSON object  {"city":"Karachi","line1":"12 Zamzama"}      -> 12 Zamzama, Karachi
          · JSON array   ["12 Zamzama","Karachi"]                     -> 12 Zamzama, Karachi
        Object keys are emitted in postal order (line1 before city before country) rather than in
        whatever order Postgres stores them, so a converted address reads like an address. Keys
        outside that list keep their relative order after the known ones. Non-string members are
        dropped: a `{"lat":24.8}` is not part of a printed address and rendering "24.8" as an
        address line would be worse than omitting it.
    -->
    <changeSet id="021-001" author="user-service">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <sqlCheck expectedResult="jsonb">
                SELECT data_type FROM information_schema.columns
                 WHERE table_schema = current_schema()
                   AND table_name = 'branches'
                   AND column_name = 'address'
            </sqlCheck>
        </preConditions>

        <!--
            THROUGH A FUNCTION, and both halves of that are forced.

            ALTER COLUMN ... USING refuses a subquery outright ("cannot use subquery in transform
            expression"), and flattening an object or an array needs one — jsonb_each and
            jsonb_array_elements are set-returning. A SQL function is the only place the subquery
            is allowed to live.

            An UPDATE-then-ALTER would have been the obvious alternative and is WRONG here:
            `branches` is FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY on app.current_tenant_id, and FORCE means the
            table's own owner — which is who Liquibase connects as — is subject to the policy too.
            With no tenant GUC set, that UPDATE matches zero rows, reports success, and the
            migration silently converts nothing. The ALTER's own table rewrite is DDL and is not
            filtered by RLS, so every tenant's rows are converted.

            Its body lives in its own .sql file so that BranchAddressMigrationIT can run THE SAME
            statement this migration runs against a fixture table, instead of asserting against a
            copy that drifts.
        -->
        <sqlFile path="db/changelog/v1.0.0/021-branch-address-jsonb-to-text-fn.sql"
                 splitStatements="false"
                 stripComments="false"/>

        <sql splitStatements="false"><![CDATA[
            ALTER TABLE branches
                ALTER COLUMN address TYPE text
                USING branch_address_jsonb_to_text(address)
        ]]></sql>

        <sql splitStatements="false"><![CDATA[
            DROP FUNCTION branch_address_jsonb_to_text(jsonb)
        ]]></sql>

        <rollback>
            <!--
                Back to jsonb as a JSON STRING, which is the only shape that round-trips: the text
                the column now holds is free-form and cannot be re-split into the object it may
                once have been. to_jsonb() on a text value produces exactly the quoted-string form
                the old column accepted, so a rolled-back row is readable by the old code.
            -->
            <sql splitStatements="false"><![CDATA[
                ALTER TABLE branches
                    ALTER COLUMN address TYPE jsonb
                    USING (CASE WHEN address IS NULL THEN NULL ELSE to_jsonb(address) END)
            ]]></sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
